<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Conwy</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Afon Conwy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The River Conwy is a river in north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, about 55 km long, that rises in the uplands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snowdonia">Snowdonia</a> and flows north through the Conwy valley to the sea at the town of Conwy. It long formed a frontier between Gwynedd west of the river and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perfeddwlad">Perfeddwlad</a> to the east, including the kingdom of Rhos. Edward I built Conwy Castle at its mouth in the 1280s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-conwy.jpg" alt="River Conwy" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Dbenbenn assumed (based on copyright claims). · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boats_in_River_Conwy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
